The engagement-governance subsystem (Rules of Engagement, Concept of Operations,
Deconfliction Plan, and the OPPLAN — Objective / OPPLAN data model, the objective
status state machine, C2Tier / OpsecLevel, and the MITRE ATT&CK kill-chain
reference) is derived from Decepticon's:

  - tools/opplan.py            (Objective, OPPLAN, ObjectivePhase, ObjectiveStatus,
                                C2Tier, OpsecLevel, and the status state machine)
  - tools/defense/conops.py    (the Concept of Operations generator)
  - middleware/roe.py          (the Rules of Engagement structure)
  - tools/references/killchain.yaml  (the ATT&CK kill-chain reference)

The data model and state machine were reconstructed and reshaped onto HackPit's
own persistence (SQLite, upsert-only, engagement-scoped) and its propose-only,
human-approval-is-the-bound execution model.

The interactive persistent-session engine (named tmux sessions with per-session
cwd/env, automatic interactive-prompt detection, the background / auto-background
lifecycle with a notify-once completion, output tiering, and the wedged-session +
tmux pipe-degradation recovery ladders) is derived from Decepticon's:

  - tools/bash/bash.py     (the tmux drive, PS1-marker completion detection,
                            pipe-pane session logging, ANSI stripping)
  - tools/bash/prompt.py   (the session semantics, the background-job lifecycle,
                            output management, and the recovery detectors)

The session MECHANICS were adopted; Decepticon's autonomy over stdin (its agent
sending `is_input` to drive an interactive prompt) was deliberately NOT adopted.
In HackPit every input path is human-only — the orchestrator can never send input.
